None of the sentences give a threshold, a defining feature of "extensive", or a contrast between a case that is and one that is not. They all restate the trigger wording. Sentence 7 does mention "large scale", but only for the other triggers (special category data and monitoring), not for extensive evaluation.

Sentences 1-6 and 8 repeat the legal text: automated processing, profiling, and legal or similarly significant effects. That is the definition of the trigger. It does not say how to judge "extensive". The "decisions with legal effects" part is a separate condition, not a test for extensiveness.

{"states": [], "partially": [], "reason": "All sentences merely restate the Article 35(3)(a) wording; none explains what makes an evaluation 'extensive' via threshold, defining feature, or contrast. 'Large scale' in sentence 7 applies to other triggers."}
